---
/* The story of how we got here, told one card at a time, with no JavaScript at all.
 *
 * The mechanism is a radio group: one hidden radio per card, and every visible control is
 * a <label> pointing at one of them. Checking a radio reveals its card through a sibling
 * selector. That is why the source order is radios first, then cards — the combinator only
 * reaches forward, so nothing may move above the inputs.
 *
 * Why not JavaScript: this site ships zero script tags, the about page says so, and a
 * LOGGER claim invites people to count the requests. A toy is not worth making three
 * honest sentences false. It also means the deck works with JS off, which the design
 * notes require of every page.
 *
 * The show/highlight rules are generated below rather than hand-written, so adding an
 * entry to curios.ts is the only edit needed. They are injected with `is:inline` because
 * Astro cannot scope a stylesheet it did not author — the ID in each selector carries
 * enough specificity to beat the scoped `display: none`.
 */
import { curios } from '../curios';

/* This took a `num` prop so the page could number its section heads — "02 — Deck",
 * "03 — Deck" depending on what sat above it. The ordinals were removed from the
 * section heads on 2026-09-20 and the prop went with them; there is nothing left for a
 * caller to pass. See DESIGN.md § The kicker. */

/* ── The starfield ──
 * Each layer is ONE element carrying a small SVG tile as its background, repeated.
 *
 * ── It was radial-gradients, and that froze the page on a real phone ──
 * The first version built each layer's background out of 8–26 stacked CSS
 * radial-gradients. It looked identical to this and it was unusable: measured on a
 * Galaxy S24 Ultra over USB, scrolling the homepage produced frames of **974–1481ms** —
 * second-long freezes, which is exactly what "it gets stuck when scrolling" felt like.
 *
 * The cost is rasterising a stack of gradients at that device's 3.75x pixel ratio, and
 * nothing tuned it away. Measured on the device, every one of these still stalled:
 * smaller tiles, two layers instead of four, `will-change: auto`, no animation at all.
 * Only removing the star layers entirely was clean.
 *
 * One SVG tile per layer is clean. Chrome rasterises the tile once and repeats the
 * bitmap, where a gradient stack is re-rasterised across the whole layer:
 *
 *     radial-gradient stars  frames= 97   max= 974ms   stalls=1
 *     SVG tile stars         frames=174   max=  25ms   stalls=0
 *     no stars at all        frames=177   max=  33ms   stalls=0
 *
 * So: if you change how these layers are drawn, measure it on a phone, not in a desktop
 * browser. A desktop at 1x never showed this at all.
 *
 * The positions are still generated from a FIXED SEED so the same sky comes out of every
 * build — a random field would re-roll on each deploy and fill the diff with noise.
 */
/* ── Star colours ──
 * Real stars are not white, and a field of one colour is the difference between a sky and
 * a spray of dots. These are deliberately close together: at one pixel across, a hue that
 * reads as "blue" in a swatch reads as "dirt" on screen, so the spread is in colour
 * temperature only — cool white through to a faint amber — and never in saturation.
 * %23 rather than # in every one: a literal hash ends a data: URL. */
const TEMPS = ['%23dff0ff', '%23e8f6ff', '%23ffffff', '%23fff4e2', '%23ffe9c9', '%23e4fff4'];

function field(
  seed: number, count: number, w: number, h: number, r: number, alpha: number,
  /* Floor for the brightness roll. The default 0.4 is the ordinary sky. The spark layer
   * passes a high floor so every star it draws clears the halo and spike thresholds
   * below — it is a handful of bright stars by definition, not a lucky roll. */
  minMag = 0.4,
) {
  let s = seed >>> 0;
  const next = () => ((s = (Math.imul(s, 1664525) + 1013904223) >>> 0) / 4294967296);
  let dots = '';
  for (let i = 0; i < count; i++) {
    const x = +(next() * w).toFixed(1);
    const y = +(next() * h).toFixed(1);
    // Vary each star's brightness, or the field reads as a regular grid of identical dots.
    const mag = minMag + next() * (1 - minMag);
    const o = alpha * mag;
    const col = TEMPS[Math.floor(next() * TEMPS.length)];

    /* ── The bright few ──
     * A sky where every star is the same size is the other half of the same problem. The
     * top of the brightness range gets a halo and, brighter still, a pair of spikes.
     *
     * THE HALO IS CONCENTRIC CIRCLES, NOT A GRADIENT, AND THAT IS NOT A STYLE CHOICE.
     * DESIGN.md § The cosmos: this field was a stack of radial-gradients once and it
     * froze a real phone for over a second at a time. Three flat circles rasterise for
     * nothing; a <radialGradient> per star walks straight back toward what was measured
     * and removed. If you want a softer falloff, add a fourth ring. */
    if (mag > 0.72) {
      dots += `<circle cx='${x}' cy='${y}' r='${(r * 3.6).toFixed(2)}' fill='${col}' opacity='${(o * 0.07).toFixed(3)}'/>`;
      dots += `<circle cx='${x}' cy='${y}' r='${(r * 2.2).toFixed(2)}' fill='${col}' opacity='${(o * 0.14).toFixed(3)}'/>`;
    }
    if (mag > 0.9) {
      /* Two hairlines crossing the star. Four-point diffraction is what a camera does to
       * a bright point, and it is the cheapest cue that this is a photograph of a sky
       * rather than a pattern. Kept under a tenth of the star's own opacity — at full
       * strength it reads as a sparkle sticker. */
      const len = (r * 7).toFixed(2);
      const sw = (r * 0.42).toFixed(2);
      dots += `<path d='M${(x - +len).toFixed(2)} ${y}H${(x + +len).toFixed(2)}M${x} ${(y - +len).toFixed(2)}V${(y + +len).toFixed(2)}' stroke='${col}' stroke-width='${sw}' opacity='${(o * 0.09).toFixed(3)}'/>`;
    }

    dots += `<circle cx='${x}' cy='${y}' r='${r}' fill='${col}' opacity='${o.toFixed(2)}'/>`;
  }
  // Single quotes inside so the whole thing can sit in a double-quoted url().
  return `url("data:image/svg+xml,<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' width='${w}' height='${h}'>${dots}</svg>")`;
}

/* Tile sizes are the loop distance as well as the texture size: each layer is one tile
 * larger than the section and drifts by exactly one tile, so the loop is seamless. */
const sky = {
  far:   { f: field(20260920, 10, 190, 150, 0.7, 0.55), w: 190, h: 150 },
  mid:   { f: field(70112358, 7,  150, 120, 0.9, 0.75), w: 150, h: 120 },
  nearA: { f: field(31415927, 5,  230, 180, 1.2, 0.95), w: 230, h: 180 },
  nearB: { f: field(27182818, 4,  200, 160, 1.1, 0.90), w: 200, h: 160 },
  /* ── The spark layer ──
   * Three bright stars on a deliberately LARGE tile, carrying the only conspicuous
   * twinkle in the sky. Everything about its size is a compromise between two failures:
   *
   *   too small a tile — the same three stars blink in step across thirty repeats, and
   *     a grid of synchronised blinks is unmistakably a texture, not a sky;
   *   too large a tile — the bitmap the browser has to hold grows with its area, and at
   *     a phone's 3.75x that gets expensive fast. 300x240 is 1125x900 device pixels.
   *
   * This is also why there is one spark layer and not three. Individual stars cannot
   * really twinkle independently while the field is a repeating tile; what sells it is
   * several layers breathing on periods that never line up, which is what the block in
   * the stylesheet does. */
  spark: { f: field(16180339, 3, 300, 240, 1.35, 1, 0.88), w: 300, h: 240 },
};

/* ── Auto-advance ──
 * Seconds each chapter holds before the deck moves itself on. The whole loop is this
 * times the number of chapters, so at 42 chapters and 10 seconds it is 7 minutes end to
 * end. Ten is about the floor for a block of two sentences — below it the deck is moving
 * before an average reader has finished, and the hover pause stops being a courtesy and
 * starts being the only way to read the thing.
 */
const DWELL = 10;
const LOOP = DWELL * curios.length;
/* A chapter's share of the loop, and the entry fade as a share of it. The fade is 640ms
 * to match .curio__card's own curio-in, so a card that arrives on the timer looks like a
 * card that arrived because you pressed the button. */
const WIN = (100 / curios.length).toFixed(6);
const FADE = ((0.64 / LOOP) * 100).toFixed(6);

  /* ── Why a sentinel radio and not "checked" on the first card ──
   * #curio-auto is a 43rd radio in the same group, checked on load, pointing at no card.
   * While it holds the check the deck is in AUTO mode; the instant a label is pressed a
   * real radio takes the check away from it and the deck is in MANUAL mode for good.
   * That is the whole state machine, and it is one selector.
   *
   * The obvious alternative — leave "checked" on card 1 and detect "some other card is
   * checked" — was tried and is subtly wrong: pressing Back from card 2 returns the check
   * to card 1, which is indistinguishable from never having pressed anything, so the deck
   * would silently start moving again under a reader who had just taken control of it.
   *
   * It also means no ":has()". The sentinel is the first element in the deck, so a plain
   * sibling combinator reaches every card. */
const rules = curios
  .map(
    (_, i) => `
#curio-${i}:checked ~ .curio__card--${i} { display: block; }
#curio-${i}:focus-visible ~ .curio__card--${i} {
  outline: 2px solid var(--accent); outline-offset: 3px;
}`,
  )
  .join('') +
  `
/* ── THE FLOOR, and it is outside the media query on purpose ──
   If the timer cannot run for any reason at all, chapter 1 is still on screen. */
#curio-auto:checked ~ .curio__card--0 { display: block; }

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
  /* ── Every card needs a box before it can be animated ──
     The first working version flipped "display" in the keyframes, and it failed in a way
     worth recording: a card whose base style is "display: none" has no box, so the
     animation that was supposed to give it a box never runs at all. Measured on the page,
     42 cards carried the animation properties and document.getAnimations() returned 1.

     So the cards stack in one grid cell and the timer moves VISIBILITY instead. This is
     the same conclusion DESIGN.md reaches for the showcase swipe, for the same reason,
     and it is worth reading that note before changing this one. What it costs is the
     usual price of a shared cell: the deck is as tall as its tallest chapter, about
     420px, rather than as tall as the one on screen. */
  #curio-auto:checked ~ .curio__card {
    display: block;
    grid-area: 1 / 1;
    visibility: hidden;
    opacity: 0;
  }
  /* A second floor, for the case where the query matches but the animation does not
     start: chapter 1 stays up rather than the deck going blank. Its own animation has
     delay 0, so it takes over on the first frame and this never flickers. */
  #curio-auto:checked ~ .curio__card--0 { visibility: visible; opacity: 1; }

 
 
