    )
    .join('\n') +
  `
  /* ── The pause, which is not a nicety ──
     Text that replaces itself on a timer while someone is halfway through reading it is
     the whole reason WCAG 2.2.2 exists. Two things answer it: hovering or tab-focusing the
     deck holds the current chapter, and pressing any control stops the timer for the rest
     of the visit. The specificity has to match the rules above or the animation shorthand
     wins and resets play-state to running — hence repeating the #curio-auto prefix. */
  .curio__deck:hover #curio-auto:checked ~ .curio__card,
  .curio__deck:focus-within #curio-auto:checked ~ .curio__card {
    animation-play-state: paused;
  }

  /* The per-card delay is POSITIVE, and it is the one number here that is easy to get
     backwards. A negative delay does not hold an animation back, it starts it already
     that far advanced — so -30s on chapter 2 put it 30s PAST its own window and the deck
     went blank after the first card.

     NOTE FOR ANYONE EDITING THIS BLOCK: it lives inside a JS template literal, so a
     backtick in one of these CSS comments closes the literal and the file stops compiling
     with a message that points nowhere near here. That already happened once. Use quotes.

     "step-end" is what makes visibility flip at the end of an interval rather than
     halfway through it. The one keyframe that overrides the timing is the fade, which
     needs a real curve. */
  @keyframes curio-cycle {
    0% {
      visibility: visible; opacity: 0; transform: translate3d(0, 12px, 0) scale(0.988);
      animation-timing-function: cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1);
    }
    ${FADE}% { visibility: visible; opacity: 1; transform: none; }
    ${WIN}%  { visibility: hidden;  opacity: 0; transform: none; }
    100%     { visibility: hidden;  opacity: 0; transform: none; }
  }
}
`;
---

<section class="curio wrap" id="how-we-got-here" aria-labelledby="curio-head">
  {/* ── The sky ──
      Decoration, and it obeys the same rules as the page's own glow and grid: it holds
      no text, it takes no pointer, and the section is complete without it. It is masked
      to fade out at every edge, so it belongs to this section rather than ending in a
      rectangle — the same treatment the page grid gets in global.css.

      Scoped to the section's own box rather than bled to 100vw. A full-bleed layer is
      the more dramatic version and it is how this page has twice grown a horizontal
      scrollbar; the mask does most of the same work with none of that risk. */}
  {/* ── The rendered nebula ──
      A picture, built by tools/render-sky.mjs and checked in. Read that file's header
      for why it is a file at all; the short version is that domain-warped noise with
      carved dust lanes is not something to ask a browser for, and DESIGN.md § The cosmos
      is emphatic about what this section may cost to paint.

      IT SITS OUTSIDE .sky ON PURPOSE, AND MOVING IT IN WILL BREAK IT SILENTLY.
      It is screen-blended, and blending only reaches an element's real backdrop while no
      ancestor isolates it. .sky carries a mask, a mask makes a stacking context, and a
      stacking context isolates the blending of everything inside it. Nested in .sky this
      element would screen against nothing, go opaque black across its dark two-thirds,
      and hide the page grid — which is the rectangle this section spent a whole round
      getting rid of. As a sibling its backdrop is the page, which is what screen wants.
      .curio is safe to sit in: position: relative with z-index auto and overflow: clip
      makes no stacking context. */}
  {/* The image is on an INNER element because .neb carries the edge-fade mask, and a
      transform on .neb would drag the mask along with it — the fade would swim about and
      the section would show its edges again. Outer element holds still and masks; inner
      element drifts. */}
  <div class="neb" aria-hidden="true"><div class="neb__drift"></div></div>

  <div class="sky" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="sky__neb"></div>
    <div class="sky__limb"></div>
    {(['far','mid','nearA','nearB','spark'] as const).map((k, i) => (
      <div
        class={`sky__layer sky__layer--${['far','mid','near-a','near-b','spark'][i]}`}
        style={`--f: ${sky[k].f}; --tw: ${sky[k].w}px; --th: ${sky[k].h}px`}
      ></div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <header>
    <div class="sec">
      {/* The heading was "One true thing" while the deck was a shuffle of unrelated facts
          and each card really was one true thing on its own. It is now a single story told
          in order, so the heading promises a story and the note gives its span.

          The note used to end "Every one says where you can check it." That was removed on
          the owner's instruction, and it costs nothing, because the sourcing was never a
          claim made up here — every card carries its own linked citation under the text,
          where a reader who wants it will look. Announcing it twice was the redundant half.
          What must NOT follow is dropping the citations themselves: curios.ts requires a
          `source` on every entry and that rule is the reason this block is allowed to sit
          on this site at all.

          Keep {curios.length}: the count is read from curios.ts so it can never drift from
          what is on the page. */}
      <h2 class="display display--sec sec__title" id="curio-head">How did we come about?</h2>
      <p class="sec__note">
        What the evidence tells us about our history.
      </p>

      {/* ── The primer ──
          Four sentences on what "science says" means, because the deck makes 42 confident
          claims about things nobody watched happen, and a reader is entitled to know on
          what basis. It sits BEFORE the cards for that reason: it frames them.

          The worked example is chapter 24, and it is chapter 24 on purpose — the primer
          should be checkable against something already on this page rather than asserting
          an anecdote of its own. It carries the same source link that chapter does.

          The two dates here were wrong in the draft this came from, and both were fixed
          rather than shipped: the earlier figure was given as 150,000 years (the accepted
          benchmark was Omo Kibish at about 195,000; Herto, at about 160,000, is the
          nearest thing to 150,000) and the Jebel Irhoud paper was given as 2019 (it is
          Hublin et al., Nature, June 2017). CLAUDE.md hard rule 1 applies to a paragraph
          about honest evidence more than to anywhere else on the site.

          The date sits in the Jebel Irhoud sentence rather than in an opening "Until 2017"
          clause, because that is the sentence making the claim — a reader should not have
          to carry a date forward from one sentence to place the event in the next. */}
      <div class="curio__primer">
        <p>
          Science is the study of nature — whatever nature turns out to be, that is what
          science reports. It changes its answer when the evidence changes, and that is the
          method working rather than failing. A conclusion later shown to be wrong was not a
          lie; it was the honest reading of everything available at the time.
        </p>
        {/* {' '} before the <em>: a line break between text and an inline element is
            collapsed away here, and the first render read "confidentlyHomo sapiens". */}
        <p>
          Chapter 24 is the example. The oldest fossils confidently called{' '}
          <em>Homo sapiens</em> were about 195,000 years old, from Omo Kibish in Ethiopia.
          Then in June 2017 remains from Jebel Irhoud in Morocco were dated to roughly
          300,000 years, and our species became a hundred thousand years older in a single
          paper. The old number never claimed nothing older existed — only that nothing
          older had been found.
        </p>
        {/* Its own line, and styled like a card's citation rather than trailing off the
            end of a sentence — every other claim in this section names its source that
            way and this one should not look like an afterthought. */}
        <p class="curio__primer-src">
          <a href="https://humanorigins.si.edu/research/whats-hot-human-origins/our-species-arose-least-300000-years-ago">Smithsonian’s Human Origins Program, Our species arose at least 300,000 years ago</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <div class="curio__deck">
    {/* The sentinel. It must stay FIRST — every auto-mode rule reaches the cards through a
        sibling combinator from here. Hidden from assistive tech and off the tab order: it
        is a piece of state, not a control, and announcing "43 of 42" helps nobody. */}
    <input
      class="curio__radio"
      type="radio"
      name="curio"
      id="curio-auto"
      checked
      tabindex="-1"
      aria-hidden="true"
    />
    {curios.map((_, i) => (
      <input
        class="curio__radio"
        type="radio"
        name="curio"
        id={`curio-${i}`}
        aria-label={`Show item ${i + 1} of ${curios.length}`}
      />
    ))}

    {curios.map((c, i) => (
      <article class={`curio__card curio__card--${i}`} data-glow>
        <p class="curio__kind">{c.kind}</p>

        <p class="curio__ask">{c.lead}</p>

        {/* Plain paragraph, not a <details>. It was behind a "Show me" toggle while the
            deck was a shuffle and `lead` was a standalone claim you could read on its own
            — the toggle held back an answer. In a 42-part story `lead` is only a chapter
            title, so the toggle was holding back the entire content of the card and every
            one of the 42 needed a click before it said anything. */}
        {c.reveal && <p class="curio__text">{c.reveal}</p>}

        <p class="curio__source">
          {c.sourceUrl ? <a href={c.sourceUrl}>{c.source}</a> : c.source}
        </p>

        {/* ── Which controls a chapter gets, and why it needs no state to decide ──
            Chapter 1 has nowhere to go back to, and chapters 1 and 2 have nothing to
            restart from — chapter 2's Back already IS the way to the start. So:

              chapter 1      Next
              chapter 2      Next, Back
              chapter 3–42   Next, Back, Start from the start

            Every one of those is decided by the index at build time and baked into that
            card's own markup, which is why this stays a static page. There is no rule
            anywhere asking "which chapter is showing" — the showing card simply carries
            the controls it should have.

            Back no longer wraps. It used to send chapter 1 round to chapter 42, which in a
            deck of unrelated facts was fine and in a story that starts at the Big Bang is
            just wrong. Next still wraps, because reaching the end and being offered the
            beginning is a different thing from falling off the front. */}
        <div class="curio__foot">
          <div class="curio__moves">
            <label class="curio__next" for={`curio-${(i + 1) % curios.length}`}>
              Next <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
            </label>
            {i >= 1 && (
              <label class="curio__back" for={`curio-${i - 1}`}>
                <span aria-hidden="true">←</span> Back
              </label>
            )}
            {i >= 2 && (
              <label class="curio__back curio__restart" for="curio-0">
                <span aria-hidden="true">↺</span> Start from the start
              </label>
            )}
          </div>
          <p class="curio__count">{i + 1} of {curios.length}</p>
        </div>
      </article>
    ))}

  </div>
</section>

<style is:inline set:html={rules}></style>

<style>
  .curio {
    margin-top: clamp(44px, 6vw, 78px);
    /* The hero links down to this section, and the header floats over the page — without
       this the jump parks the heading underneath the bar. --head-h is the bar plus its
       inset; the rest is the same breathing room the hero gives itself. */
    scroll-margin-top: calc(var(--head-h) + 14px);
    position: relative;
    /* Nothing inside .sky may reach the page. The layers are deliberately larger than
       this box so their drift never exposes an edge, and without this they would widen
       the document — the same bug the hero's bloom and the app head's backdrop both
       caused before they were clipped. `clip`, not `hidden`: hidden makes this a scroll
       container, which would break the focus ring the deck relies on. */
    overflow: clip;
    padding-block: clamp(30px, 4vw, 54px);
  }

  /* ---------- the cosmos ---------- */
  /* The edge fade is defined once and worn by both layers. Custom properties are
     substituted at the point of use, so the percentages inside resolve against whichever
     element applies them — both happen to be inset: 0 on .curio, so they agree. */
  .sky,
  .neb {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    pointer-events: none;

    /* ── Why three masks and not one ──
       This was a single radial — radial-gradient(118% 104% at 50% 42%, #000 28%,
       transparent 100%) — described in this comment as fading "to nothing at every
       edge". It did not. Its 100% stop is an ellipse 118% of the width and 104% of the
       height, so the stop only lands out past the CORNERS; along the edges themselves
       the mask is still nearly opaque. Measured on a 1660x629 section:

           left 0.80   right 0.80   top 0.83   bottom 0.61

       The section is `overflow: clip`, so those figures are exactly the problem: the
       starfield was cut off at 80% opacity, which draws a visible rectangle around the
       whole block — the thing this mask exists to prevent.

       The fix is to INTERSECT the radial with a horizontal and a vertical linear fade,
       each of which genuinely reaches zero at its edge. The radial keeps the vignette;
       the two linears guarantee the four edges. Order matters only for readability —
       intersect is commutative.

       The extra stops inside each linear fade are an ease. A plain two-stop gradient
       ramps alpha linearly, and the eye finds the kink where the ramp begins; these
       approximate a smoothstep so the fade has no detectable start.

       ── The ramps are clamped lengths, not bare percentages ──
       Hiding a ramp takes a roughly FIXED distance — about 55-70px — because that is a
       property of the eye, not of the layout. A bare percentage does not give you that:
       9% is a comfortable 150px on this desktop section and a useless 32px on a 360px
       phone, where the edge would quietly come back. So each ramp is a clamp() with a
       px floor for narrow screens, a percentage in the middle, and a px ceiling so a
       tall section does not fade away its own middle. The eased stops are fractions of
       the ramp rather than fixed percentages, so they scale with it.

       Top and bottom differ on purpose. The top edge meets the hero's flat black and the
       stars there are crisp, so it needs the longer ramp. The bottom edge has only the
       soft green glow to hide. A long bottom ramp erased that glow altogether, which is
       how this first came out — see the note on .sky__neb's centre. */
    --ramp-x: clamp(56px, 9%, 170px);
    --ramp-top: clamp(64px, 16%, 120px);
    --ramp-bot: clamp(44px, 11%, 90px);

    --fade-x: linear-gradient(to right,
      transparent 0,
      rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.18) calc(var(--ramp-x) * 0.28),
      rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.62) calc(var(--ramp-x) * 0.62),
      #000 var(--ramp-x),
      #000 calc(100% - var(--ramp-x)),
      rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.62) calc(100% - var(--ramp-x) * 0.62),
      rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.18) calc(100% - var(--ramp-x) * 0.28),
      transparent 100%);
    --fade-y: linear-gradient(to bottom,
      transparent 0,
      rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.18) calc(var(--ramp-top) * 0.28),
      rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.62) calc(var(--ramp-top) * 0.62),
      #000 var(--ramp-top),
      #000 calc(100% - var(--ramp-bot)),
      rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.62) calc(100% - var(--ramp-bot) * 0.62),
      rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.18) calc(100% - var(--ramp-bot) * 0.28),
      transparent 100%);
    --vignette: radial-gradient(118% 104% at 50% 42%, #000 28%, transparent 100%);

    -webkit-mask-image: var(--fade-x), var(--fade-y), var(--vignette);
    mask-image: var(--fade-x), var(--fade-y), var(--vignette);
    /* Without this the layers COMPOSITE AS `add`, which is a union — the result would be
       more opaque than any one of them and the edges would come back worse than before.
       The -webkit- line is the legacy spelling of the same operation, kept for older
       WebKit; both are widely supported now, and every browser that runs this site
       honours one of them. */
    -webkit-mask-composite: source-in;
    mask-composite: intersect;
  }

  .neb {
    /* The image is opaque and already multiplied down onto black; screen turns that back
       into light. Black adds nothing, so the page grid survives underneath. */
    mix-blend-mode: screen;
  }
  .neb__drift {
    position: absolute;
    /* Oversized by 4% on every side so the drift below always has somewhere to go. The
       cloud must never pull its own edge into view — same reasoning as the star tiles
       being one tile larger than the section. */
    inset: -4%;
    /* cover, so the crop changes with the section's shape instead of the cloud stretching
       — a stretched cloud is instantly readable as a stretched picture. */
    background: url('/assets/sky/nebula.webp') center / cover no-repeat;
  }

  /* The nebula does NOT move, and that is deliberate. DESIGN.md § Motion: a pulsing or
     drifting glow is the one change to this design that would make it look cheap. The
     stars drift; the light they are in holds still. */
  .sky__neb {
    position: absolute;
    inset: -30% -10%;
    background:
      radial-gradient(38% 52% at 22% 18%, color-mix(in oklab, var(--accent) 16%, transparent) 0%, transparent 70%),
      radial-gradient(46% 60% at 82% 72%, rgba(90, 150, 200, 0.10) 0%, transparent 72%),
      /* Centred at 92%, not 100%. .sky__neb is inset -30% top and bottom, so its own
         100% is a long way BELOW the section's bottom edge — the glow's peak used to sit
         189px outside the box and only its upper skirt was ever visible. That was fine
         while the mask cut the bottom off at 0.61 alpha and hid the difference; once the
         mask actually fades to zero down there, the skirt faded with it and the green
         went out of the section entirely. Raising the centre puts the bright part back
         inside the region the mask keeps. */
      radial-gradient(70% 80% at 50% 92%, var(--glow-soft) 0%, transparent 76%);
  }

  /* A planet's limb, mostly below the fold of the section: one enormous circle with a
     hairline edge. It is the cheapest cue there is that this is space rather than a
     dark panel with dots on it, and it costs one element and no motion. */
  .sky__limb {
    position: absolute;
    left: 50%;
    top: 62%;
    width: 178%;
    aspect-ratio: 1;
    translate: -50% 0;
    border-radius: 50%;
    border: 1px solid color-mix(in oklab, var(--accent) 13%, transparent);
    background: radial-gradient(closest-side, color-mix(in oklab, var(--accent) 4%, transparent) 0%, transparent 72%);
  }

  /* One element per depth, each tiled and each drifting at its own speed — which is the
     whole of the parallax. The element is a full tile larger than the section in both
     axes, and the animation translates it by exactly one tile, so the loop is seamless
     and no edge is ever exposed. Change --tw/--th and the keyframe follows automatically. */
  .sky__layer {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    left: 0;
    width: calc(100% + var(--tw));
    height: calc(100% + var(--th));
    background-image: var(--f);
    background-size: var(--tw) var(--th);
    background-repeat: repeat;
    /* No `will-change: transform`. It was here, and measured on the device it bought
       nothing — 174 frames with it, 173 without, the same 25ms worst frame — while
       permanently promoting four full-section layers into GPU memory. An animated
       transform is promoted by the compositor anyway. */
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    /* Slow enough that you never catch it moving — you only notice it has moved.
       ── On the periods ──
       Every one of these is a different number and none is a multiple of another. That is
       the whole trick: five layers breathing on periods that never line up read as a sky
       with depth in it, where any two in step read as the page blinking. If you retune
       them, keep them mutually awkward — 7 and 14 would be a mistake, 7 and 9.5 are not.

       EACH ELEMENT GETS EXACTLY ONE `animation` DECLARATION, with its effects separated
       by commas. DESIGN.md § Motion: a second `animation` shorthand on the same element
       wins outright and silently deletes the first, which has already cost this codebase
       one bug. */
    .sky__layer--far    { animation: sky-drift 280s linear infinite, sky-twinkle 23s ease-in-out -5s infinite; }
    .sky__layer--mid    { animation: sky-drift 190s linear infinite, sky-twinkle 17s ease-in-out -11s infinite; }
    .sky__layer--near-a { animation: sky-drift 130s linear infinite, sky-twinkle 7s ease-in-out infinite; }
    .sky__layer--near-b { animation: sky-drift 104s linear infinite, sky-twinkle 9.5s ease-in-out -3.2s infinite; }
    /* The spark layer drifts slowest and blinks hardest — it is the only place in the
       sky where a single star visibly winks. Deeper than the rest on purpose; at the
       others' amplitude it would not read as a star at all. */
    .sky__layer--spark  { animation: sky-drift 340s linear infinite, sky-spark 5.5s ease-in-out -1.7s infinite; }

    /* ── The nebula drifts, and this is a rule that was reversed ──
       DESIGN.md § The cosmos said "the nebula does not move", and § Motion called a
       drifting glow the one change that would make this design look cheap. The owner
       asked for it on 2026-09-21; both notes now record that, and this is the
       implementation they describe.
       The restraint is in the numbers. 240 seconds for one circuit of about 1.5% of the
       section's width — a full loop takes four minutes and the peak speed is under a
       pixel a second. You cannot catch it moving; leave the tab a while and the cloud is
       somewhere else. The one thing that would earn the original objection is speed.
       It is a CLOSED LOOP, not an alternate. An alternating drift has to stop and turn
       around, and the turnaround is the moment the eye catches — which is exactly the
       tell that made a moving glow look cheap in the first place. */
    .neb__drift { animation: neb-drift 240s linear infinite; }

    @keyframes sky-drift {
      from { transform: translate3d(0, 0, 0); }
      to   { transform: translate3d(calc(var(--tw) * -1), calc(var(--th) * -1), 0); }
    }
    @keyframes sky-twinkle {
      0%, 100% { opacity: 1; }
      50%      { opacity: .55; }
    }
    @keyframes sky-spark {
      0%, 100% { opacity: 1; }
      50%      { opacity: .18; }
    }
    /* Four corners of a slow ellipse, returning exactly to the start so the loop has no
       seam. Percentages are of this element's own box, which is the oversized one. */
    @keyframes neb-drift {
      0%   { transform: translate3d(-0.9%, -0.5%, 0) scale(1.015); }
      25%  { transform: translate3d(0.5%, -0.9%, 0)  scale(1.03); }
      50%  { transform: translate3d(0.9%, 0.5%, 0)   scale(1.02); }
      75%  { transform: translate3d(-0.5%, 0.9%, 0)  scale(1.005); }
      100% { transform: translate3d(-0.9%, -0.5%, 0) scale(1.015); }
    }
  }

  /* Everything real sits above the sky. */
  .curio > header,
  /* A grid so the auto-advance can stack every chapter in one cell — see the note by
     #curio-auto in the generated rules. In manual mode exactly one card is display:block,
     so this is a one-item grid and lays out exactly as the old block did. */
  .curio__deck { position: relative; z-index: 1; display: grid; }

  /* Capped to a reading measure rather than stretched across the page: a card running
     twice as wide as the sentence introducing it reads as an accident.

     This used to carry `margin-left: 196px` — the 148px metadata rail plus its 48px gap —
     so that the deck lined up with the section title rather than with the page edge. The
     rail is gone and the title is flush left now, so the indent that once aligned them
     would be the one thing on the page that is not. */
  /* Full measure, not 760px. The card was the last block on the catalogue page that
     stopped short of the right edge while everything above it reached it — see the
     note in apps/index.astro about why that raggedness read as mess. The measure that
     matters is the one on the TEXT inside, which is capped separately below; the card
     around it may be as wide as the page. */
  .curio__deck { margin-top: clamp(28px, 4vw, 44px); }

  /* Focusable but invisible: the Back and Another one labels are the visible controls, and
     the card itself takes the focus ring when its radio is focused. Never display:none
     here — that would take the whole group out of the tab order and off the keyboard,
     which is what makes arrow keys walk the deck. */
  .curio__radio {
    position: absolute;
    width: 1px; height: 1px;
    margin: -1px; padding: 0; border: 0;
    clip-path: inset(50%);
    overflow: hidden; white-space: nowrap;
  }

  /* The 3px accent left border is gone. A white rounded card with a coloured stripe
     down one side is the alert component every utility framework ships, and it was
     spending the page's one accent on something that is not clickable. */
  /* Frosted, not solid — and here that is not a style choice, it is the reason the sky
     was worth drawing. DESIGN.md § The atmosphere: a glass panel over a flat opaque
     surface is just a grey box, and it only reads as glass when there is something
     behind it. There is now, so the starfield and the nebula come through the card and
     the panel stops looking like a rectangle sitting on a picture. */
  /* ── The hover glow ──
     data-glow is the site's own mouse-follow edge, already built and already served:
     /motion.js writes --gx/--gy for any [data-glow] element it finds, so this costs no
     new script at all. global.css draws the ring and the spill; everything below only
     rescales them, because that implementation is sized for a bento tile and this card
     is up to 1660px wide — a 240px circle on it reads as a torch, not as an edge
     catching light.

     With JavaScript off, on a phone, or under reduced motion, --gx/--gy stay at their
     50%/50% default and the card still lights up on hover, just evenly. Nothing here is
     hidden behind the script.

     ── It is also the pause indicator, and that is the real reason it earns its place ──
     Hovering the deck stops the 10-second timer. Without a visual answer, a reader who
     rests the pointer on a card has no way to know the thing has stopped waiting to move
     — so the glow is what says "this is yours now, read it". Hover state and timer state
     are the same state, which is why one signal can honestly stand for both.

     ── Do not reach for transform here ──
     The obvious extra polish is a lift on hover. It cannot work: in auto mode this
     element is running curio-cycle, which sets transform in its keyframes, and an
     animation beats a normal declaration no matter the specificity. The hover would be
     silently ignored for the showing card and apply only in manual mode, which is worse
     than not having it. box-shadow and border-color are untouched by the keyframes,
     which is why the lift here is made of light instead of movement. */
  .curio__card {
    display: none;
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--hairline);
    border-radius: var(--r-xl);
    padding: clamp(22px, 3vw, 34px);
    transition:
      box-shadow 320ms var(--ease),
      border-color 320ms var(--ease);
  }
  /* Scaled for the card. The radii are the only numbers changed from global.css. */
  .curio__card::before {
    background: radial-gradient(
      460px circle at var(--gx) var(--gy),
      var(--accent) 0%,
      color-mix(in oklab, var(--accent) 35%, transparent) 40%,
      transparent 74%
    );
  }
  .curio__card::after {
    background: radial-gradient(
      620px circle at var(--gx) var(--gy),
      var(--glow-soft) 0%,
      transparent 72%
    );
  }
  /* The bloom the ring sits in. Three shadows rather than one: a hairline that tightens
     the edge, a wide cast that lifts the card off the sky, and a very soft halo that is
     mostly there to stop the second one ending in a visible line. All of them are mint
     at low alpha, so the card looks lit by the section it is sitting in rather than by a
     spotlight that arrived from nowhere. */
  .curio__card:hover,
  .curio__card:focus-within {
    border-color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--accent) 26%, var(--hairline));
    box-shadow:
      0 0 0 1px color-mix(in oklab, var(--accent) 10%, transparent),
      0 20px 64px -26px color-mix(in oklab, var(--accent) 38%, transparent),
      0 0 110px -34px color-mix(in oklab, var(--accent) 30%, transparent);
  }
  @supports ((backdrop-filter: blur(1px)) or (-webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(1px))) {
    .curio__card {
      background:
        linear-gradient(to bottom, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.05), rgba(255, 255, 255, 0) 34%),
        color-mix(in oklab, var(--surface) 62%, transparent);
      -webkit-backdrop-filter: var(--glass-blur);
      backdrop-filter: var(--glass-blur);
    }
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    /* The card animates in every time it is shown, and the mechanism is free: going
       from `display: none` to `display: block` makes an element newly rendered, and a
       newly rendered element starts its animations from the beginning. So pressing
       "Another one" replays this with no script and no state to track.

       One `animation` shorthand only — DESIGN.md § Motion records a section head that
       silently stopped moving because a second shorthand overwrote the first. */
    .curio__card { animation: curio-in 640ms var(--ease-out) both; }
    @keyframes curio-in {
      from { opacity: 0; transform: translate3d(0, 12px, 0) scale(0.988); }
      to   { opacity: 1; transform: none; }
    }
  }

  .curio__primer {
    margin-top: 18px;
    max-width: 68ch;
    font-size: 14.5px;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  .curio__primer p { margin: 0 0 9px; }
  .curio__primer p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
  .curio__primer em { font-style: italic; }
  .curio__primer-src { margin-top: 10px !important; font-size: 13px; opacity: .85; }

  .curio__kind {
    margin: 0 0 16px;
    font-family: var(--font);
    font-size: 13px; font-weight: 500; letter-spacing: 0.005em;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .curio__ask {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 44ch;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: clamp(20px, 2.5vw, 28px);
    font-weight: 400; letter-spacing: var(--track-tight); line-height: 1.2;
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }
  /* The chapter itself. This replaced a <details> and about thirty lines of disclosure
     styling — a custom marker, a rotate on open, and a @media (pointer: coarse) bump to
     get "Show me" over the 44px touch floor. None of it is needed by a paragraph. */
  .curio__text {
    margin: 18px 0 0;
    max-width: 66ch;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .curio__source {
    margin: 20px 0 0;
    font-size: 13px; color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  .curio__source a { color: inherit; text-decoration-color: var(--hairline); }

  .curio__foot {
    margin-top: 20px;
    display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 16px; flex-wrap: wrap;
  }
  .curio__next {
    display: inline-block;
    cursor: pointer;
    font-size: 15px; font-weight: 700;
    padding: 10px 16px;
    border: 1px solid var(--hairline);
    border-radius: var(--r-md);
    background: var(--ground);
  }
  .curio__next:hover { border-color: var(--accent); }
  .curio__count { margin: 0; font-size: 13px; color: var(--ink-soft); }

  /* This started as a row of dots, one per card, which works for a short deck and stops
     working the moment it is not one: at twenty-one entries the row is 924px of targets,
     wrapping into three rows of mostly nothing on a laptop. Two moves and a counter say
     the same thing in a fixed amount of space, however long the deck gets. */
  .curio__moves { display: flex; gap: 10px; flex-wrap: wrap; }
  .curio__back {
    display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px;
    min-height: 44px;
    padding: 0 14px;
    cursor: pointer;
    font-size: 15px; font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    border: 1px solid transparent;
    border-radius: var(--r-md);
  }
  .curio__back:hover { color: var(--ink); border-color: var(--hairline); }
  /* Third in a row of three, and the one you want least often — so it is the quietest.
     Same shape as Back, a step further back in the hierarchy. */
  .curio__restart { font-weight: 500; opacity: .78; }
  .curio__restart:hover { opacity: 1; }
</style>
